  there's usually some interesting  psychology there and in this case  it really boils down to fear in a lot of  instances basically how Donald Trump  uses fear to keep that support strong  and this has to do with how the brain  responds to fear and our behavioral  response to fear so I can talk about  each of those things yes let's talk  about a term that people may not be  familiar with this terror management  theory and when people hear that they  might think that that's related to sort  of like law enforcement and preventing  terrorist attacks or something like that  but it actually comes from a book in the  70s called the denial of death and it  has to do with exactly what you're  talking about how human behavior is sort  of influenced by psychological phenomena  and how we respond to what politicians  say and do so let's let's focus in on  that first if we can how does that work  yes so terror management theory is kind  of complicated but when you understand  it it seems to explain like everything  that we're seeing right now as far as  like Donald Trump's unwavering support  and this growing nationalist movement  we're seeing not just in America but  globally and when I say tear it has to  do with existential terror we're talking  about fear of death or anything that  seems to be a threat to either you as an  individual or a threat to your way of  life so basically the theory is based on  as you said Blitzer Prize winning book  from the 70s called the denial of death  written by a cultural anthropologist  named Ernest Becker and Becker's big  idea was that humans have a unique  awareness of their own mortality so  unlike other animals we know that some  day we're going to die and probably be  forgotten and every trace of our  existence will be erased from history  which makes it kind of hard to go  through life so he believes that are  believed I should say that humans  created cultural worldviews worldviews  like religions political ideologies  national identities to ease  this constant fear of death that's kind  of always bubbling beneath the surface  and worldviews help mitigate this fear  of death by giving paths to immortality  and by that I mean religions provide a  path to literal immortality through the  concept of an afterlife while political  ideologies and national identities give  us what's called symbolic immortality  and that really refers to feeling like  we're part of a larger group and  identity and a movement that will  outlive the individual so worldviews  instill life with meaning and value and  distract from our fear of death and make  us feel like life's worth living there  basically death anxiety buffers so what  the theory predicts the hypothesis it  makes is that when people think about  existential fear or any sort of threat  or when they're subjected to scary  messages like fear-mongering what they  do to defend against that is that they  strengthen their cultural worldviews  since we're all views protect us from  this fear we tend to become more  ideologically extreme when we're fearful  and we tend to put more support into  those who share our worldviews and  become more aggressive toward those with  opposing world views  so basically existential fear makes us  behave more tribally and more hostile  toward those from outside cultures okay  so Melissa you make the case that this  is particularly noticeable with Trump  and to provide some context a lot of  what you're saying I wasn't missing from  President Obama so and it's different  but for example when you talk about  climate change and the existential  threat that climate changes maybe not to  us but to future generations by  participating in a fight against climate  change you are pushing back in  exactly the way you describe sort of  something bigger than ourselves  preserving the planet for future  generations that's just one example  right so what I would like to hear is  how is this more acute or pronounced  under the Trump sort of doctrine so to  speak yes so anytime you have  existential fear it's going to have this  effect the effect isn't always  predictable sometimes it pushes both  Republicans and Democrats towards the  right depending on the type of  existential threat other times it makes  people more extreme ideologically toward  whatever side they're already on so  things you know constantly talking about  the threat of climate change is going to  do that but that's a real threat so that  is something that we need to worry about  so you can't just not talk about a  threat because you're worried about the  effects that it might have if the  threats real on the other hand you know  with Donald Trump a lot of these threats  are exaggerated and that's really where  the problem is and the way he says it if  you if you watch some of the footage  where he's talking about there's a CNN  interview with Anderson Cooper saying  that Islam hates America and he just  says it over and over again and he said  that uh you know rally speech that Obama  was the founder of Isis he reiterated  that like five times for the audience so  when you have this existential fear  people are going to become more  supportive of you especially they become  more supportive of leaders with  nationalist messages and you know the  threat is real I mean Isis is real it's  something you know to be afraid of so  Donald Trump kind of used that to his  advantage and I think the the fears  around Isis kind of set the stage for  Donald Trump and cultural fears over  immigration in general kind of set the  stage for him but as far as like the  evidence for this term management theory  is supported by  hundreds of psychology neuroscience  studies that show that you know making  people think about existential fear  makes them more nationalistic and  creates like racial biases and so for  some specific examples we saw that after  9/11 George Bush went from being a  pretty unpopular president to a very  popular one another example of leader  with the nationalist messages becoming  our gaining support and a tear  management that study explored that and  showed that existential fear was related  to increased support for Bush a similar  study was done with Donald Trump and  this was conducted by the co-founder of  terror management theory so basically  they found that when people were made to  think of existential threat they became  more supportive of Donald Trump and  these same people viewed immigrants  moving into their neighborhood as an  existential threat Bobby to touch on one  of the other sort of pillars of what  we're talking about here in the sort of  limited time we have I'd like to talk  about the relationship of what we've  discussed so far the use of fear  existential threats etc and how that  interacts with the sort of entertainment  value of the way that Donald Trump has  run for president and has been president  so he holds people's attention between  the insults and the bombastic nature and  everything else in a way that Hillary  Clinton uniformly did not this is not a  commentary on their policies it's merely  an assessment of the way in which from  an entertainment perspective Donald  Trump was able to influence people can  you talk about how that relates to this  concept of unconditional support or  support that goes beyond the facts yes  so the way Donald Trump speaks and  gestures engages the brain more than  other politicians do when they're  speaking and that's something we know  from you know science studies we see  specifically the areas of the brain  involved in attention and emotion  are activated more strongly and for  longer times when Donald Trump is  speaking a company called narrow spark  did a study sparked neural actually they  did a study where they had participants  democrats and republicans watched like  an hour of footage of debates and  speeches right before the 2016  presidential election and they saw this  pattern where Donald Trump was  consistently engaging those brain areas  compared to Hillary Clinton who wasn't  really good at all at keeping the brain  engaged what was really interesting  about it is some people mentioned you  know not really being fans of Donald  Trump and they weren't intending to vote  for him but their brains were telling  this different story they were they were  showing like a lot of activation in  attention areas and when they followed  up with these people  months later some of them were Democrats  they found out you know some of them  confessed to actually voting for Trump  because ultimately they said you know  his messages resonated with them so  there seems to be something about the  way Trump talks I'm sure alive it has to  do with like simple language like you  said like no bombastic comments and just  his overall way of engaging people  probably learned that from being you  know a reality show host but he engages  everyone you know they're their brains  more strongly than other politicians the  reason that this is relates to the  unwavering support so this effect is is  amplified in conservatives because  conservatives have this hypersensitivity  to threat generally speaking so by that  I mean they tend to focus on threat more  and they tend to have this exaggerated  fear response to threatening messages so  we know this from a number of different  studies for example one study took  sir motives and liberals and had them  sit in front of a computer screen where  they showed a bunch of different images  some of the images were threatening  somewhere neutrals some are positive and  they track their eye movements and what  they found is that conservatives fixated  on the threatening images longer and  they oriented toward the threatening  images more quickly then liberals so  yeah we call that being hyper-vigilant  for threat and a couple other studies  showed that conservatives tend to have a  larger amygdala and a more reactive  amygdala in response to threat yes oh  the amygdala is a brain structure that  is involved in processing threat and  it's also associated with the fear  response  so when Donald Trump is saying these  scary messages their brains are engaged  even more strongly his messages are more  salient because they're in a way tuned  into threat and I'm not really trying to  pick on conservatives here that's what  the studies show also you know someone  could interpret that differently and you  could see it as Republicans or  conservatives might also be better  equipped to respond to a threat in the  case that you know something does happen  because they're they're hyper vigilant  absolutely fascinating stuff we've been  speaking with cognitive neuroscientist  Bobby Azarian who also blogs for  Psychology Today you can follow him on  twitter at bobby Azarian and check out  his new youtube channel road to omega  Bobby really a pleasure having you on  party to be here thanks David anytime

